WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.267
-Our next guest
is an award-winning

00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:03.300
investigative journalist

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.601
and staff writer
for "The New Yorker."

00:00:04.601 --> 00:00:07.100
Please welcome to the show
Jane Mayer, everyone.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:14.267
♪♪

00:00:14.267 --> 00:00:19.467
♪♪

00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:21.501
-Jane, thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.167
-Thank you.
Good to be with you.

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:25.067
-You are an
investigative journalist.

00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:26.267
You cover dark money.

00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:28.801
You cover corruption
in politics.

00:00:28.801 --> 00:00:32.934
Do people panic when they
get a phone call from you?

00:00:32.934 --> 00:00:35.734
-I have read
that people have said

00:00:35.734 --> 00:00:38.033
that when they hear
a message from me,

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:40.300
they call their solicitor
and get their,

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.801
you know, affairs in order.

00:00:42.801 --> 00:00:45.434
It's not the most flattering
thing to read about yourself,

00:00:45.434 --> 00:00:46.534
but I have read that.

00:00:46.534 --> 00:00:47.968
-But then what do you do?

00:00:47.968 --> 00:00:49.300
So I would assume
a lot of people

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.834
initially don't want
to talk to you.

00:00:50.834 --> 00:00:52.501
I would assume you don't
give up at that point.

00:00:52.501 --> 00:00:54.000
Who do you reach out to next

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.601
when the sort of, I don't know,
subject of a story won't talk?

00:00:57.601 --> 00:01:00.467
-Yeah, so rule one
is never give up, okay?

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:01.601
And what do you do?

00:01:01.601 --> 00:01:03.534
You have to report
around them sometime.

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:06.767
And, you know,
you get desperate.

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:09.801
Some of the times -- One of
the things that's worked for me

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:12.133
is I found a lot
of great information

00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:14.200
from calling people's families.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.267
-Oh, yeah.

00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:17.067
-Especially people's mothers.

00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:18.133
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:19.868
-If I wouldn't talk to you

00:01:19.868 --> 00:01:21.467
but then I heard
you were calling my mother,

00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:23.767
I would immediately
call you back.

00:01:23.767 --> 00:01:25.200
-It usually works like that.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.801
But sometimes
you can get to the mother

00:01:26.801 --> 00:01:28.701
before the person
calls you back,

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:30.901
and then you get just, like,
a mother lode.

00:01:30.901 --> 00:01:32.167
-Yeah, there you go.

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.033
That's how they originally
came up with the name.

00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:35.534
-It must be, yeah.

00:01:35.534 --> 00:01:39.501
-So you've reported a lot about
the fight for abortion rights.

00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:41.400
You have a big piece coming up.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.133
Obviously I think a lot of
people maybe weren't expecting

00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:47.200
the Kansas voters to reject

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.767
this rollback of reproductive
rights last night.

00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:53.300
Were you surprised, and what
do you see moving forward?

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.200
-Well, so I've been watching it
really closely.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.801
I actually have been talking
to Democratic operatives,

00:01:58.801 --> 00:02:02.334
including James Carville,
who said a few weeks ago,

00:02:02.334 --> 00:02:04.267
"This is the one.
You got to watch it.

00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:08.701
Because if the pro-choice people
win in Kansas,

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:12.367
it's just gonna rock the
Republicans back on their heels

00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:14.567
and it's gonna
energize the Democrats."

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:17.300
And he wasn't entirely sure
what was going to happen,

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.868
but he said
he felt good about it.

00:02:18.868 --> 00:02:21.934
So I had a little bit
of advance notice on the thing.

00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:27.000
It is amazing because Kansas
is obviously a very red state.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.501
I mean, and I think --

00:02:28.501 --> 00:02:31.534
I mean, to me, what it means
is we're sort of getting --

00:02:31.534 --> 00:02:34.634
we're in the consequences phase
of politics

00:02:34.634 --> 00:02:37.133
where you're beginning
to see the consequences

00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:40.767
of right-wing rule
that's more extreme

00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:42.701
than most people in the country.

00:02:42.701 --> 00:02:44.334
-Well, it was
a surprising result,

00:02:44.334 --> 00:02:48.067
but it wasn't surprising in that
all the polling you ever see

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.834
shows that most people
wanted Roe to stand.

00:02:50.834 --> 00:02:52.234
-Well, this is true.

00:02:52.234 --> 00:02:55.801
But it's just -- I mean, and
that's so -- it's a great point.

00:02:55.801 --> 00:03:00.300
Because most of these states, if
you look at the general public,

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:04.000
they support things like access
to abortion within reason,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.534
you know, legal abortion.

00:03:05.534 --> 00:03:09.334
And they support
restrictions on guns.

00:03:09.334 --> 00:03:13.133
They actually support
higher taxes on the super rich.

00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:16.100
I mean, all these things
that never get passed.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.567
But what's interesting
is the state legislatures

00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:22.667
are far more extreme
than the people themselves.

00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:25.734
-You have written a lot
about dark money in politics,

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:27.367
corruption in politics,

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:30.334
and recently have been
doing some writing

00:03:30.334 --> 00:03:31.334
about the Supreme Court.

00:03:31.334 --> 00:03:33.067
And obviously the Supreme Court

00:03:33.067 --> 00:03:37.734
as an idea is held, you know,
with this very great esteem

00:03:37.734 --> 00:03:40.868
and yet not immune to dark money

00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:43.701
and influence
from outside forces.

00:03:43.701 --> 00:03:45.434
Do you think people
are surprised

00:03:45.434 --> 00:03:47.334
when they read pieces of yours

00:03:47.334 --> 00:03:50.067
insofar as they didn't think
that this was possible

00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:52.200
of happening
to the Supreme Court?

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.367
-I think so.

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:55.267
I mean, I think all of us
grew up revering it

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.000
and thinking
it's above politics.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.234
But if you look at polls now,
it's at a low ebb

00:04:00.234 --> 00:04:03.234
for popular trust
in the Supreme Court,

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:05.667
and I think it's because people
are beginning to see it

00:04:05.667 --> 00:04:10.100
as so political
and taking such extreme stances.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.133
I think it's that,
and then you're right.

00:04:12.133 --> 00:04:14.367
There is money
that people don't realize

00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:17.567
is going into confirming
these justices.

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:21.267
Millions of dollars have gone
into dark money groups

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:24.000
that have pushed for these
very far right justices.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.334
And most of us reporters,
including myself,

00:04:27.334 --> 00:04:30.467
still don't know where
those millions are coming from.

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:32.267
It's almost impossible
to figure out.

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:34.033
But I'm going to try.

00:04:34.033 --> 00:04:35.968
-So you --
Well, we're glad you're trying.

00:04:35.968 --> 00:04:37.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.834
You wrote a -- You wrote
a fascinating piece

00:04:41.834 --> 00:04:44.868
about Clarence Thomas's wife,
Ginni Thomas,

00:04:44.868 --> 00:04:48.133
and went out of your way,
which I think everyone does,

00:04:48.133 --> 00:04:51.334
saying, like, "Look, obviously,
you know, everyone's spouse

00:04:51.334 --> 00:04:53.968
has a right to, you know,
have their own pursuits.

00:04:53.968 --> 00:04:56.501
And you don't judge one
for the other."

00:04:56.501 --> 00:05:00.534
With that said,
she is very deep in advocacy

00:05:00.534 --> 00:05:02.868
and she is deep in issues
and organizations

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:05.968
that are bringing cases
in front of the Supreme Court,

00:05:05.968 --> 00:05:07.701
funding -- what are they called?

00:05:07.701 --> 00:05:09.501
Amicus?
-Amicus briefs.

00:05:09.501 --> 00:05:10.667
-Amicus briefs.
-Right.

00:05:10.667 --> 00:05:12.501
-And sort of
friend of the court briefs

00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:15.067
that are supporting
their position, and --

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:18.667
and yet Clarence Thomas is not
recusing himself in these cases.

00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:21.033
Doesn't seem like he has
any intention of doing that.

00:05:22.968 --> 00:05:24.267
Where does this all end?

00:05:24.267 --> 00:05:26.501
Is it basically that the
Supreme Court is just allowed

00:05:26.501 --> 00:05:27.968
to police themselves in a way

00:05:27.968 --> 00:05:29.734
that other judges aren't
at lower levels?

00:05:29.734 --> 00:05:31.634
-Well, that's the thing
that's sort of amazing.

00:05:31.634 --> 00:05:33.434
It's the highest court
in the country,

00:05:33.434 --> 00:05:35.033
and it has
the lowest ethics bar.

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:36.701
It just polices itself.

00:05:36.701 --> 00:05:38.767
The rest of the judges
in the country

00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:40.801
are bound by a judicial code,

00:05:40.801 --> 00:05:43.901
but they decided that
the Supreme Court is the highest

00:05:43.901 --> 00:05:45.501
and nobody
can tell them what to do.

00:05:45.501 --> 00:05:47.100
So they self-police.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.300
I mean, any other judge
would have recused themselves

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.400
if they were faced
with the situation

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:53.734
that Clarence Thomas was.

00:05:53.734 --> 00:05:56.234
There's actually a law
about recusal

00:05:56.234 --> 00:05:58.033
which says that every judge,

00:05:58.033 --> 00:06:00.667
including justices
on the Supreme Court,

00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:04.400
are supposed to recuse
from any case

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.901
in which a spouse has a material
interest in the outcome.

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:12.133
We're waiting to see Ginni's
whole role in this,

00:06:12.133 --> 00:06:15.734
but it certainly seems that she
was up to her eyeballs in it

00:06:15.734 --> 00:06:18.400
and would be very uncomfortable
for Clarence Thomas

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:21.501
to be impartial
in a case like that.

00:06:21.501 --> 00:06:22.767
-And I think we've all --

00:06:22.767 --> 00:06:24.067
Certainly with
the super majority, right,

00:06:24.067 --> 00:06:25.901
we're getting used to 6-3 cases

00:06:25.901 --> 00:06:28.534
in the same way
we got used to 5-4 cases.

00:06:28.534 --> 00:06:30.901
But there was actually
an 8-1 case

00:06:30.901 --> 00:06:34.934
where Clarence Thomas was the
only justice to, you know --

00:06:34.934 --> 00:06:36.501
that stood alone.

00:06:36.501 --> 00:06:37.868
And this was the one --

00:06:37.868 --> 00:06:40.467
If there was any one to recuse
himself, from this one,

00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:42.701
because this was turning over
text messages from January 6th,

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:43.968
and we obviously know
that Ginni Thomas

00:06:43.968 --> 00:06:45.734
was texting with Mark Meadows
at the time.

00:06:45.734 --> 00:06:48.734
-She was,
and the texts are doozies,

00:06:48.734 --> 00:06:50.200
if anyone's had a chance
to see them.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:54.167
-To use the legal term,
they are doozies.

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:57.767
-So, yeah, it's an incredibly --

00:06:57.767 --> 00:07:00.400
It's not just
a sticky situation.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.901
It's really a serious situation,

00:07:02.901 --> 00:07:05.601
because if you,
the American public,

00:07:05.601 --> 00:07:07.934
lose faith in the Supreme Court

00:07:07.934 --> 00:07:10.934
in a country where the rule
of law is everything,

00:07:10.934 --> 00:07:13.167
things can really
deteriorate fast.

00:07:13.167 --> 00:07:15.300
We need to revere
the Supreme Court

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.033
and we need to have transparency

00:07:17.033 --> 00:07:19.501
so that we know
who's taking money

00:07:19.501 --> 00:07:22.501
and who's got conflicts
of interest up there.

00:07:22.501 --> 00:07:24.000
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.901
-The other thing
that's fascinating to me

00:07:26.901 --> 00:07:31.434
is, you know, justices
like Clarence Thomas now,

00:07:31.434 --> 00:07:33.067
it seems like
when they give speeches,

00:07:33.067 --> 00:07:36.934
they blame the rest of us for
politicizing the Supreme Court.

00:07:36.934 --> 00:07:40.234
And this was even before, you
know, we knew the full thrust

00:07:40.234 --> 00:07:44.100
of Ginni's involvement
in January 6th.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:47.934
Do you find -- Is that level
of hypocrisy stunning to you,

00:07:47.934 --> 00:07:51.234
or is it what you've
come to expect?

00:07:51.234 --> 00:07:54.434
-Well, I think sometimes
it's almost like projection.

00:07:54.434 --> 00:07:56.968
You hear from these people
an accusation

00:07:56.968 --> 00:07:59.868
that the press is politicizing
the Supreme Court,

00:07:59.868 --> 00:08:03.467
and, you know, it makes you want
to take a second look

00:08:03.467 --> 00:08:06.634
'cause it's clear it's a little
closer to home in his case.

00:08:06.634 --> 00:08:10.834
The politics is intense in his
breakfast table, I would think.

00:08:10.834 --> 00:08:13.501
-Yeah, you -- Another thing
that I would imagine

00:08:13.501 --> 00:08:15.434
makes you take a closer look
at things

00:08:15.434 --> 00:08:17.400
is names of a lot
of these organizations

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.567
are, like, the Honest Elections
Project or FreedomWorks.

00:08:20.567 --> 00:08:23.501
When they seem to have, like,
those sort of mundane names,

00:08:23.501 --> 00:08:24.801
is that when you're like,

00:08:24.801 --> 00:08:26.601
"Oh, there's some real
dirty business here?"

00:08:26.601 --> 00:08:29.033
-[ Laughs ]
It's true.

00:08:29.033 --> 00:08:31.734
Like, the more virtuous
the title,

00:08:31.734 --> 00:08:34.534
the more sort of mucky stuff

00:08:34.534 --> 00:08:36.167
is usually going on
under the surface.

00:08:36.167 --> 00:08:39.000
But it's helpful,
'cause it's like a red flag.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:42.501
It's like, "Oh, I better take
a second look at this one."

00:08:42.501 --> 00:08:45.868
-Now, is it true that before you
were investigative journalist,

00:08:45.868 --> 00:08:48.100
your dream job
at "The New Yorker"

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:50.200
at a time
was to be a cartoonist?

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:51.300
-It actually was.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:52.767
That's what I really
wanted to be.

00:08:52.767 --> 00:08:54.434
And the first thing
I ever submitted

00:08:54.434 --> 00:08:55.834
to "The New Yorker"
was a cartoon.

00:08:55.834 --> 00:08:57.234
-And did you hear back?
-I did.

00:08:57.234 --> 00:09:01.000
I got sort of a polite form note
saying, "Thanks, try again."

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:02.501
[ Laughter ]

00:09:02.501 --> 00:09:06.100
-Did you feel like you had
a skill with a pen?

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:07.801
Were you a good artist?
-I loved cartooning.

00:09:07.801 --> 00:09:10.400
-I did it all through college,
yeah, and it was a lot of fun.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:11.667
-You weren't just, like,
jumping into it.

00:09:11.667 --> 00:09:13.434
You had some background in it.
-Yeah.

00:09:13.434 --> 00:09:16.167
I just couldn't figure out
how you make a living at it.

00:09:16.167 --> 00:09:18.234
And the thing that happens
at "The New Yorker"

00:09:18.234 --> 00:09:20.200
is they pay you by the word.
-Yeah.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:24.167
Oh, so it's way better to do
long investigative journalism

00:09:24.167 --> 00:09:27.033
than, like,
some real pithy caption.

00:09:27.033 --> 00:09:29.300
-The more complicated,
the better.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:32.033
-So I want to ask --
end by asking,

00:09:32.033 --> 00:09:33.367
obviously investigative
journalists

00:09:33.367 --> 00:09:36.434
are portrayed in television
and film a great deal,

00:09:36.434 --> 00:09:38.100
oftentimes makes it
look thrilling,

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:39.467
like a thrilling job
that you have.

00:09:39.467 --> 00:09:42.734
How accurate would you say
those portrayals are?

00:09:42.734 --> 00:09:44.133
-Wishful thinking.

00:09:44.133 --> 00:09:47.801
You know, sometimes you feel
like you're from that movie

00:09:47.801 --> 00:09:48.968
"A Beautiful Mind."

00:09:48.968 --> 00:09:50.601
You know, I have a whiteboard

00:09:50.601 --> 00:09:52.234
with all these things
scribbled on it.

00:09:52.234 --> 00:09:54.767
And if anyone walked in,
they'd bring in, like, you know,

00:09:54.767 --> 00:09:58.033
one of those butterfly nets
to take you away, I think.

00:09:58.033 --> 00:09:59.834
It's har-- It's --

00:09:59.834 --> 00:10:02.868
But it truly is exciting
when you get a breakthrough,

00:10:02.868 --> 00:10:04.767
and you do get them,
and it's like --

00:10:04.767 --> 00:10:07.934
they are real Eureka moments,
and it's kind of incredible.

00:10:07.934 --> 00:10:09.033
-Goose bumps?

00:10:09.033 --> 00:10:10.367
When you realize
you have a breakthrough,

00:10:10.367 --> 00:10:11.834
are you like, "Oh, my God"?

00:10:11.834 --> 00:10:15.000
-No, you're like, "Thank you!"

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:18.000
-Well, we're the beneficiaries
of your breakthroughs, as well.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.033
Thank you so much
for being here.

00:10:20.033 --> 00:10:22.634
Looking forward
to your next article.

00:10:22.634 --> 00:10:23.968
Jane Mayer, everybody!

